WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.499
How constrained decoding works, in one paragraph of math.

2
00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:08.536
Recall from lesson 1 that each step produces a probability for every token.

3
00:00:08.816 --> 00:00:13.957
With a grammar, the system tracks which state of the automaton the output so far has reached.

4
00:00:14.237 --> 00:00:23.112
At each step it sets the probability of every token that would leave the language of valid documents to zero, renormalizes the rest, and samples.

5
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:30.944
If your schema says the next thing must be one of “high”, “medium”, or “low”, tokens that start any other string are masked out.

6
00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:34.275
Two consequences follow, and both matter for trust.

7
00:00:34.555 --> 00:00:40.165
First, the output is valid by construction: you can’t get a missing brace or an unknown key.

8
00:00:40.445 --> 00:00:45.075
Second, the model must choose among the options you allowed, even if none of them fits.

9
00:00:45.355 --> 00:00:52.203
Masking redistributes probability over the legal tokens; it doesn’t create a “this doesn’t apply” option.

10
00:00:52.483 --> 00:01:02.723
If the transcript never states a priority and your enum has no way to say so, the model will pick one, and the result will look exactly as confident as a real answer.

11
00:01:03.003 --> 00:01:08.443
That’s why schema design, not just the feature, decides whether structured output is honest.
